WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(intro music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.666
(audience clapping)

00:00:01.666 --> 00:00:02.499
- Nice to meet you.

00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:03.332
- All right, where do I sit?

00:00:03.332 --> 00:00:04.165
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:00:04.165 --> 00:00:07.198
- [Kelly] You're right here, come on over.

00:00:07.198 --> 00:00:08.031
- Welcome back,

00:00:08.031 --> 00:00:08.864
do you know Henry?

00:00:08.864 --> 00:00:09.697
Have y'all ever met?

00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:10.530
- I don't know you,

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.745
I don't have the pleasure.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:12.578
But you were in the first Scream.

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:14.151
- I was, I was in the first Scream.

00:00:14.151 --> 00:00:15.524
- You were the principal.

00:00:15.524 --> 00:00:16.607
- The principal.

00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:18.037
- And you had an epic death.

00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:18.870
- I did!

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:19.703
- [Kelly] I know, I was gonna say

00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:20.536
he didn't make the next one.

00:00:20.536 --> 00:00:21.369
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:22.202
- No.

00:00:22.202 --> 00:00:23.035
The director said to me,

00:00:23.035 --> 00:00:23.868
When you're stabbed could it be

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.752
a little more excruciating?

00:00:25.752 --> 00:00:27.043
I said, Yes, it could.

00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:27.982
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:29.550
- And you, and in that scene

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.511
when you're like, you're hearing things

00:00:31.511 --> 00:00:32.782
you open the door and it's

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:33.719
Wes Craven mopping the floor.

00:00:33.719 --> 00:00:34.641
(audience whooping)

00:00:34.641 --> 00:00:36.266
- Absolutely.

00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:37.099
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:37.932
I love that you know

00:00:37.932 --> 00:00:38.981
the knowledge of the first one.

00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:40.154
- Congratulations to you.

00:00:40.154 --> 00:00:40.987
- Thank you so much.

00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:41.996
- [Melissa] And to you.

00:00:41.996 --> 00:00:42.829
- Thank you.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:44.412
- Well, congrats on the new movie, Carmen.

00:00:44.412 --> 00:00:45.245
- Thank you.

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.078
- So this is incredible.

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:46.911
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:00:46.911 --> 00:00:47.744
- Thank you.

00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:48.577
- So is it based on the opera?

00:00:48.577 --> 00:00:49.883
- [Melissa] It is, it's very, very loosely

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.207
based on the opera.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:52.040
- Okay.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:52.873
- It is,

00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:53.928
- What does that mean?

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:54.823
I'm curious.

00:00:54.823 --> 00:00:55.922
- Basically, it is nothing

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.279
like the opera except for,

00:00:57.279 --> 00:00:58.112
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:58.112 --> 00:00:59.905
- [Melissa] except for the character.

00:00:59.905 --> 00:01:00.738
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:01:00.738 --> 00:01:01.571
- You know, like the essence

00:01:01.571 --> 00:01:02.404
of the character

00:01:02.404 --> 00:01:03.739
and the energy of Carmen is there

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:06.113
- but the story is completely different.

00:01:06.113 --> 00:01:09.346
It's like a modern retelling and,

00:01:09.346 --> 00:01:10.781
- Maybe a prequel to like

00:01:10.781 --> 00:01:12.393
maybe who she was before.

00:01:12.393 --> 00:01:13.332
- You know what?

00:01:13.332 --> 00:01:15.056
That's what I say,

00:01:15.056 --> 00:01:16.735
but I don't know that that's the truth.

00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:17.580
But in my mind it's

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.363
kind of like an origin story.

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:20.356
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:20.356 --> 00:01:21.557
- I like that.

00:01:21.557 --> 00:01:23.225
- [Melissa] Yeah. I like it, too..

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:24.058
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:01:24.058 --> 00:01:25.574
- Alright, well you connected

00:01:25.574 --> 00:01:28.253
with the story in a
personal way, so how so?

00:01:28.253 --> 00:01:29.499
- Well, I'm Mexican and this

00:01:29.499 --> 00:01:31.312
(audience clapping)

00:01:31.312 --> 00:01:32.145
thank you.

00:01:32.145 --> 00:01:35.260
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.341
And I, so when I moved to the US

00:01:37.341 --> 00:01:39.528
I would only, almost only, exclusively

00:01:39.528 --> 00:01:41.576
get offers for, or like

00:01:41.576 --> 00:01:43.880
get auditions for either playing

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.405
like drug cartel related stuff

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.397
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:48.168
Or like immigration related stuff.

00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:50.099
And I said to my team from the beginning,

00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:51.805
I was like I'm not going to go out

00:01:51.805 --> 00:01:52.638
for those roles

00:01:52.638 --> 00:01:54.105
because there's so much of that,

00:01:54.105 --> 00:01:55.650
and it's not all that we are.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.483
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:57.316
(audience clapping)

00:01:57.316 --> 00:01:58.149
- And then, and then

00:01:58.149 --> 00:01:59.636
- Absolutely.

00:01:59.636 --> 00:02:02.097
- But then when Carmen came along,

00:02:02.097 --> 00:02:04.189
it was such an interesting,

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:05.955
like beautiful

00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:07.138
dance,

00:02:07.138 --> 00:02:08.971
like epic romantic way

00:02:10.553 --> 00:02:12.439
of telling an immigration story

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:13.570
that I was like, I don't,

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.350
I've never seen it done like this.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.441
It's a woman that's like

00:02:17.441 --> 00:02:19.024
running from danger

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:22.355
across the border

00:02:22.355 --> 00:02:24.601
to find a better life.

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.568
And along the way she falls in love

00:02:26.568 --> 00:02:28.776
with this guy that, you know,

00:02:28.776 --> 00:02:31.035
is actually trying to prevent her

00:02:31.035 --> 00:02:31.974
from crossing the border

00:02:31.974 --> 00:02:33.277
but then they end up
running away together. And

00:02:33.277 --> 00:02:35.533
- Oh, I like that tension.

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:36.366
- Yeah. Right?

00:02:36.366 --> 00:02:37.199
- Yeah.

00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:38.747
- And it's Paul Mescal.

00:02:38.747 --> 00:02:39.643
- I like that.

00:02:39.643 --> 00:02:40.506
- I mean

00:02:40.506 --> 00:02:41.339
- Okay. Okay.

00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:44.073
- But so, when I, when I heard,

00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:46.293
when I saw the script
and Benjamin Millepied,

00:02:46.293 --> 00:02:47.893
our incredible director's vision,

00:02:47.893 --> 00:02:51.089
I was like maybe this is a different way

00:02:51.089 --> 00:02:52.959
of telling a story and getting through

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:54.656
to people about like

00:02:54.656 --> 00:02:56.116
The real life necessity

00:02:56.116 --> 00:02:57.667
that these people are going through,

00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:58.821
and the dangers in a very like,

00:02:58.821 --> 00:03:00.654
romantic, musical way.

00:03:02.088 --> 00:03:03.501
- Yeah. It's the best way is art.

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:04.334
- Yeah.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.352
- In general, is to teach people

00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:06.387
and educate people.

00:03:06.387 --> 00:03:07.220
- Yeah.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.053
- I agree with that.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:08.886
Do you, Henry, do you have

00:03:08.886 --> 00:03:10.286
a real personal connection

00:03:10.286 --> 00:03:11.556
with like, any of your characters

00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:13.115
you've ever played?

00:03:13.115 --> 00:03:15.022
- I think that every great character

00:03:15.022 --> 00:03:16.105
is inside us.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:17.773
Man or woman.

00:03:17.773 --> 00:03:18.606
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:03:18.606 --> 00:03:22.261
- And our job is to find that kernel,

00:03:22.261 --> 00:03:24.485
and then you add on the
condition of your life

00:03:24.485 --> 00:03:25.977
and your imagination.

00:03:25.977 --> 00:03:27.243
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:03:27.243 --> 00:03:28.910
- What a stellar answer!

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.743
- I've always felt,

00:03:29.743 --> 00:03:31.110
I don't know if you feel like this.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.969
Because I'm not the type of actor

00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:34.861
that can like,

00:03:34.861 --> 00:03:36.292
disappear into a role

00:03:36.292 --> 00:03:38.127
because my way into a character

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:40.068
is always my personal connection to them.

00:03:40.068 --> 00:03:41.914
And to me, every character that I play

00:03:41.914 --> 00:03:44.960
is like a version of Melissa,

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.917
if I had been born under
these circumstances

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:48.985
at this time period.

00:03:48.985 --> 00:03:51.257
- And that is why you are authentic.

00:03:51.257 --> 00:03:53.285
Even when you're sitting here talking,

00:03:53.285 --> 00:03:55.728
you just seem to be having a conversation

00:03:55.728 --> 00:03:57.253
with the audience and Kelly.

00:03:57.253 --> 00:03:58.086
- Yeah.

00:03:58.086 --> 00:03:58.919
(audience clapping)

00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:00.251
- I like how you say Melissa.

00:04:00.251 --> 00:04:01.509
(Melissa chuckling)

00:04:01.509 --> 00:04:03.353
- I loved, I saw in the teleprompter my,

00:04:03.353 --> 00:04:04.273
my last name, Barrera,

00:04:04.273 --> 00:04:06.339
- But we're, is it rolled Rs,

00:04:06.339 --> 00:04:07.172
generally, though?

00:04:07.172 --> 00:04:08.005
- (rolling Rs) Barrera.

00:04:08.005 --> 00:04:08.838
- [Kelly] (rolling Rs) Barrera.

00:04:08.838 --> 00:04:09.671
- Yeah.

00:04:09.671 --> 00:04:10.504
- [Kelly] I would've done that!

00:04:10.504 --> 00:04:11.337
- Yeah, you can.

00:04:11.337 --> 00:04:12.170
- I know.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:13.256
But, I always ask people how you do it,

00:04:13.256 --> 00:04:14.195
because I hate,

00:04:14.195 --> 00:04:15.827
that's my biggest pet peeve.

00:04:15.827 --> 00:04:17.502
But I never knew it was my pet peeve

00:04:17.502 --> 00:04:18.504
before this show.

00:04:18.504 --> 00:04:19.347
- [Melissa] Yeah.

00:04:19.347 --> 00:04:21.214
- But it's like everybody
tries to American,

00:04:21.214 --> 00:04:22.047
Not you.

00:04:22.047 --> 00:04:23.069
I just mean people

00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:24.028
- [Melissa] Yeah.

00:04:24.028 --> 00:04:25.373
- trying to Americanize names

00:04:25.373 --> 00:04:26.515
to like make it in.

00:04:26.515 --> 00:04:27.955
I'm like, we're a melting pot

00:04:27.955 --> 00:04:29.436
filled with many different cultures.

00:04:29.436 --> 00:04:30.955
Like we can all say Melissa,

00:04:30.955 --> 00:04:32.375
- [Melissa] But you're a musician.

00:04:32.375 --> 00:04:33.811
You have good ear.

00:04:33.811 --> 00:04:34.703
That's why you can. I find that

00:04:34.703 --> 00:04:36.616
- [Kelly] Well then all you musicians,

00:04:36.616 --> 00:04:37.490
you can do it.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.323
(audience responding)

00:04:38.323 --> 00:04:39.156
- Like, it's like I just, it's not

00:04:39.156 --> 00:04:40.227
but I think if we're trained

00:04:40.227 --> 00:04:41.314
from childhood to like say,

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.830
Melissa is beautiful.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:43.663
It sounds beautiful.

00:04:43.663 --> 00:04:44.657
- [Melissa] Right. Thank you.

00:04:44.657 --> 00:04:45.490
- It sounds lyrical.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.323
Like I love it.

00:04:46.323 --> 00:04:47.156
- It's like a beautiful melody.

00:04:47.156 --> 00:04:47.989
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:04:47.989 --> 00:04:48.822
Next time I'm introducing you that way.

00:04:48.822 --> 00:04:49.655
- [Melissa] Right.

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:50.490
- I didn't know or I would've.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.687
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:51.687 --> 00:04:54.045
Anyway, we need to do
another commercial break.

00:04:54.045 --> 00:04:55.186
But you can see Melisa

00:04:55.186 --> 00:04:57.085
in Scream Six out now

00:04:57.085 --> 00:04:58.613
and in Carmen, which hits theaters

00:04:58.613 --> 00:05:00.543
in Los Angeles and New York April 21st,

00:05:00.543 --> 00:05:02.296
and nationwide in May.

00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:03.129
I cannot wait to see it.

